# Target C-II — upper-block structure sharpens the FREE square-root fork

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; supersedes the preceding continuous thin/thick count optimization  
**Scope:** all-inactive non-anchored FREE reflection ancestry  
**Dependencies:** two-sided inactive gate absorption; two-sided outer-face localization; exact upper-event backtracking identity; sharp superdiameter-thrackle theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_block_optimized_sqrt_tradeoff_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding optimized square-root theorem still paid for every upper gate separately
when counting how many lower runs can occur. That is unnecessary. Consecutive upper
gates form one block and create only one break between lower runs. If a block contains
no thick upper gate, it contains a thin edge that can be charged to the thin-edge
matching. This changes the denominator in the face-run averaging from roughly
`m+2M` to `m+M`, where `m` is the number of thick upper gates and `M` is the maximum
matching size among thin upper gates.

The result is a substantially larger common square-root constant.

Put

\[
h_*={\sqrt{303}\over52}=0.334747984337100\ldots
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
 c_{\rm blk}
 :=\left(1+{h_*\over2}\right)^{-1/2}
 =0.925539574680581\ldots .
}
\tag{BO.1}

Then every all-inactive non-anchored FREE ancestry has at least one of the following
outcomes.

### BLOCK-SQRT-FACE

One consecutive all-lower run in the original outer contact face has support span

\[
\boxed{
 \operatorname{span}_N(R)
 \ge(c_{\rm blk}-o(1))\sqrt D.
}
\tag{BO.2}

For every fixed `Lambda>1`, it contains a vertex-disjoint matching of old fully inactive
outer-face source pairs of size

\[
\boxed{
 \left({\Lambda-1\over2\Lambda}c_{\rm blk}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D
}
\tag{BO.3}

with

\[
\boxed{
1<|x-y|
\le
1+\left({\Lambda h_*\over c_{\rm blk}}+o(1)\right)D^{-1/2}.
}
\tag{BO.4}

For `Lambda=2`, this is a matching of size

\[
\boxed{
(0.231384893670145\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D
}
\tag{BO.5}

with excess at most

\[
\boxed{
(0.723357473833849\ldots+o(1))D^{-1/2}.
}
\tag{BO.6}

### BLOCK-SQRT-SUPER-BRAID

There is a vertex-disjoint matching of old fully inactive source pairs of size

\[
\boxed{
(c_{\rm blk}-o(1))\sqrt D
}
\tag{BO.7}

and every retained edge is strictly longer than

\[
\boxed{\sqrt{D^2-1}.}
\tag{BO.8}

Hence the chords cross properly pairwise and the existing superbraid
forest-transversal theorem applies unchanged.

The previous continuous count optimization gave the common coefficient
`0.679255...`. The only new input here is the exact one-dimensional block structure of
the activation ancestry; the metric backtracking estimate itself is unchanged.

---

## 1. Thin and thick upper edges

Use the exact sharp threshold

\[
\tau_D:=D-\sqrt{D^2-1}
={1\over D+\sqrt{D^2-1}}.
\]

An upper gate with deficit `rho` is **thin** if `rho<tau_D` and **thick** otherwise.
Every thin edge has original length

\[
D-\rho>\sqrt{D^2-1}.
\]

Let

- `m` be the number of thick upper edges;
- `M` be the size of a maximum vertex-disjoint matching among all thin upper edges of
  the ancestry;
- `B` be the number of maximal consecutive blocks consisting of upper edges;
- `R` be the number of maximal consecutive all-lower runs.

Because the ancestry is a path in edge order, any matching of thin upper edges is a
source matching. The sharp superdiameter theorem therefore makes all `M` selected thin
edges pairwise properly crossing.

---

## 2. The upper-block inequality

### Lemma BO.1

\[
\boxed{B\le m+M.}
\tag{BO.9}

### Proof

Split the maximal upper blocks into two classes.

1. A block containing at least one thick edge is charged to one such thick edge. Since
   the blocks are edge-disjoint, distinct blocks receive distinct thick edges. There
   are at most `m` blocks of this kind.
2. A block containing no thick edge consists entirely of thin edges. The maximum thin
   matching on the whole ancestry restricts blockwise, and every nonempty pure-thin
   block contributes at least one matching edge. Equivalently, the maximum matching on
   the disjoint union of the pure-thin blocks has size at least the number of those
   blocks. Hence there are at most `M` pure-thin blocks.

Adding the two classes proves (BO.9). `square`

Deleting the upper blocks leaves at most one more lower block than upper blocks, so

\[
\boxed{R\le B+1\le m+M+1.}
\tag{BO.10}

This is the improvement over the preceding theorem. Counting individual upper edges
would only give `R<=U+1`, while a long consecutive upper run causes only one lower-run
break.

---

## 3. Thick-edge backtracking is unchanged

For an upper gate with activation increment `Delta`, deficit `rho`, and support
backtracking `b`, the exact first-hit identity gives

\[
2\Delta b=2D\rho-\rho^2+\Delta^2,
\]

hence

\[
b\ge{\rho\over\Delta}\left(D-{\rho\over2}\right).
\]

Set

\[
\bar t_D=h_*+D^{-1/3},
\]

\[
A_D=D\tau_D\left(1-{\bar t_D\over2D}\right),
\qquad
k_D={A_D\over\bar t_D}.
\tag{BO.11}

The activation-time budget is at most `bar t_D`. Cauchy over the `m` thick upper gates
therefore gives

\[
\boxed{B_{\rm back}\ge k_Dm^2.}
\tag{BO.12}

Moreover

\[
\boxed{k_D\longrightarrow k_\infty={1\over2h_*}.}
\tag{BO.13}

The support ledger consequently gives total positive lower-edge support progress

\[
\boxed{
G_{\rm low}\ge D+k_Dm^2-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{BO.14}

---

## 4. Exact minimax balance with block count

Define

\[
\boxed{
 c_D:=\left(1+{1\over4k_D}\right)^{-1/2}.
}
\tag{BO.15}

Then

\[
c_D\longrightarrow
\left(1+{h_*\over2}\right)^{-1/2}
=c_{\rm blk}.
\]

If

\[
M\ge(c_D-o(1))\sqrt D,
\]

we have BLOCK-SQRT-SUPER-BRAID immediately.

Otherwise

\[
M\le c_D\sqrt D+o(\sqrt D).
\tag{BO.16}

The definition (BO.15) is exactly equivalent to

\[
1-c_D^2={c_D^2\over4k_D}.
\]

Therefore, for every `m>=0`,

\[
\begin{aligned}
&D+k_Dm^2-c_D\sqrt D\,(m+c_D\sqrt D)\\
&\qquad=
 k_D\left(m-{c_D\sqrt D\over2k_D}\right)^2
 \ge0.
\end{aligned}
\tag{BO.17}

Combining (BO.10), (BO.14), (BO.16), and (BO.17), one lower run has support span at
least

\[
\begin{aligned}
{G_{\rm low}\over R}
&\ge
{D+k_Dm^2-O(\sqrt D)
 \over m+c_D\sqrt D+o(\sqrt D)+1}\\
&\ge(c_D-o(1))\sqrt D.
\end{aligned}
\tag{BO.18}

The numerator error contributes only `O(1)` after division because the displayed
denominator is `Omega(sqrt(D))`; if the true number of lower runs is smaller, the
average only increases. This proves BLOCK-SQRT-FACE.

---

## 5. Near-unit matching on the face run

Let the selected lower run have `r` edges and edge slacks

\[
\sigma_e=|e|-1>0.
\]

The activation-time ledger gives

\[
\sum_{e\in R}\sigma_e\le h_*+D^{-1/3}.
\]

Also support progress per edge is at most `1+sigma_e`, hence

\[
r\ge(c_{\rm blk}-o(1))\sqrt D.
\]

For fixed `Lambda>1`, Markov leaves at least `(1-1/Lambda)r` edges with

\[
\sigma_e
\le
\left({\Lambda h_*\over c_{\rm blk}}+o(1)\right)D^{-1/2}.
\]

Any set of `g` edges of a path contains a matching of size at least `g/2-O(1)`. This
proves (BO.3)--(BO.4). The `Lambda=2` constants are

\[
{c_{\rm blk}\over4}=0.231384893670145\ldots,
\]

\[
{2h_*\over c_{\rm blk}}=0.723357473833849\ldots .
\]

---

## 6. Consequences and remaining seam

The superbraid branch inherits every previously proved forest-transversal conclusion:
its endpoints are external full-hull vertices, every braid chord crosses every actual
diameter edge, the diameter graph is a caterpillar forest, and each diameter-tree
bipartition lies in complementary braid cells.

The face branch is stronger as well. Since every lower gate has length below `sqrt(2)`
for large `D`, the recent short-edge theorem places the whole run in the common plane
source-pair graph `P_<sqrt(2)(A)`.

This does not close C-II. It removes another artificial constant loss. The current
per-module FREE obstruction is now either a planar outer-face near-unit run of
`0.925539... sqrt(D)` support span or an equally large exact-threshold inactive
superdiameter braid. The next step should use multi-module planarity or the forest
outer-walk currencies, not another fixed-fraction thin/thick split.

**Disposition:** used maximal upper blocks rather than individual upper gates to sharpen
the common FREE square-root coefficient from `0.679255...` to
`0.925539574680581...`.
